// @run
// @log 1e+23
// @log true
// @log false
// @log 8.98846567431158e+307
// @log 2.225073858507201e-308
// @log 5e-324
// @log true
// @log 1.7976931348623157e+308
// @log 0
// @log 0
// A scientific literal is CORRECTLY ROUNDED to the nearest f64 — one rounding, on
// the exact rational `mantissa * 10^(exp - fracDigits)` built in bignum, never a
// `mantissa * 10^exp` product in f64. The naive product is wrong by an ULP or more
// at every value below: composing `5e-324` by dividing 5.0 by 10^324 in f64 gives
// ZERO, and `1.7976931348623157e308` composed by multiplication lands one ULP low.
//
// `print` renders an f64 in its SHORTEST ROUND-TRIPPING form, so each `@log` here
// pins one exact double: a one-ULP error changes the printed digits.
print(1e23) // 99999999999999991611392, not 10^23 — 10^23 has no f64
print(1e23 == 99999999999999991611392.0) // the double it rounds to
print(1e23 == 100000000000000008388608.0) // ... and NOT the next double up
print(8.98846567431158e307) // 2^1023, the top binade
print(2.2250738585072011e-308) // just under DBL_MIN — the classic strtod trap
print(5e-324) // the smallest positive subnormal
print(5e-324 > 0.0)
print(1.7976931348623157e308) // DBL_MAX
print(1e-400) // underflows to +0, no diagnostic
print(0e999999) // a zero mantissa stays zero at any exponent
